From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:56:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 174/342] libroot: rw_lock waits survive signals, without
 losing their wake-up

A signal to a thread waiting for an rw_lock made _kern_block_thread()
return B_INTERRUPTED, and the kernel kept that as the thread's wait
status. rw_lock_wait() blocked again, which returned at once (the wait
status wasn't positive): it spun. And when the lock was handed to it,
_kern_unblock_thread() did nothing (it only wakes a thread whose wait
status is positive), while rw_lock_unblock() had already counted the
waiter as a reader or made it the holder: the thread spun forever and
the lock was never free again. BLooperList's lock is one: a MediaPlayer
whose menu window was being deleted spun in ~BWindow and every thread
needing the looper list waited for good.

Waiters now say whether the lock was handed to them (under the lock's
mutex); after an interruption a waiter that wasn't re-arms its wait
status and blocks again. pthread_rwlock didn't lose the hand-over (its
waiters have that flag already) but spun while interrupted: it re-arms
too. tools/probes/rwlocksignal.cpp in the project shows both.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/libroot/os/locks/rw_lock.cpp       | 27 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../libroot/posix/pthread/pthread_rwlock.cpp  |  5 ++++
 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/system/libroot/os/locks/rw_lock.cpp b/src/system/libroot/os/locks/rw_lock.cpp
index bc8c6fc7b3..30a2c5b021 100644
--- a/src/system/libroot/os/locks/rw_lock.cpp
+++ b/src/system/libroot/os/locks/rw_lock.cpp
@@ -16,6 +16,10 @@ typedef struct rw_lock_waiter {
 	rw_lock_waiter *	next;
 	thread_id			thread;
 	bool				writer;
+	bool				queued;
+		// false once the lock is handed over (or destroyed): under the
+		// lock's mutex, it says whether the thread was woken
+	status_t			status;
 } rw_lock_waiter;
 
 
@@ -26,6 +30,8 @@ rw_lock_wait(rw_lock *lock, bool writer)
 	waiter.thread = find_thread(NULL);
 	waiter.next = NULL;
 	waiter.writer = writer;
+	waiter.queued = true;
+	waiter.status = B_OK;
 
 	if (lock->waiters != NULL)
 		lock->last_waiter->next = &waiter;
@@ -41,6 +47,19 @@ rw_lock_wait(rw_lock *lock, bool writer)
 	status_t result;
 	do {
 		result = _kern_block_thread(0, 0);
+		if (result == B_INTERRUPTED) {
+			// A signal: the kernel left B_INTERRUPTED as our wait status,
+			// and it only wakes a thread whose wait status is positive, so
+			// a wake-up from now on would be lost (and we'd spin here
+			// forever, counted as the lock's holder). Unless the lock was
+			// handed to us meanwhile, wait again.
+			mutex_lock(&lock->lock);
+			if (!waiter.queued)
+				return waiter.status;
+					// locked, as below
+			get_user_thread()->wait_status = 1;
+			mutex_unlock(&lock->lock);
+		}
 	} while (result == B_INTERRUPTED);
 
 	// and lock it again before returning
@@ -66,6 +85,7 @@ rw_lock_unblock(rw_lock *lock)
 
 		lock->waiters = waiter->next;
 		lock->holder = waiter->thread;
+		waiter->queued = false;
 		_kern_unblock_thread(waiter->thread, B_OK);
 		return;
 	}
@@ -73,6 +93,7 @@ rw_lock_unblock(rw_lock *lock)
 	while (waiter != NULL && !waiter->writer) {
 		lock->reader_count++;
 		lock->waiters = waiter->next;
+		waiter->queued = false;
 		_kern_unblock_thread(waiter->thread, B_OK);
 		waiter = lock->waiters;
 	}
@@ -105,8 +126,12 @@ __rw_lock_destroy(rw_lock *lock)
 
 	rw_lock_waiter *waiter = lock->waiters;
 	while (waiter != NULL) {
+		rw_lock_waiter *next = waiter->next;
+			// the waiter may go once it's woken
+		waiter->status = B_ERROR;
+		waiter->queued = false;
 		_kern_unblock_thread(waiter->thread, B_ERROR);
-		waiter = waiter->next;
+		waiter = next;
 	}
 
 	mutex_destroy(&lock->lock);
diff --git a/src/system/libroot/posix/pthread/pthread_rwlock.cpp b/src/system/libroot/posix/pthread/pthread_rwlock.cpp
index c7db457137..81d4e3c5a1 100644
--- a/src/system/libroot/posix/pthread/pthread_rwlock.cpp
+++ b/src/system/libroot/posix/pthread/pthread_rwlock.cpp
@@ -217,6 +217,11 @@ private:
 
 			if (!waiter.queued)
 				return waiter.status;
+
+			// a signal left B_INTERRUPTED as the wait status: blocking again
+			// would return at once, and spin until we're unqueued
+			if (status == B_INTERRUPTED)
+				waiter.userThread->wait_status = 1;
 		} while (status == B_INTERRUPTED);
 
 		// we're still queued, which means an error (timeout, interrupt)
